\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 Phase 3: Replication & Quorum Consensus\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf0 Step 0: Concepts you need before writing code\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Coordinator + preference list.
\f2\b0  In Phase 2, a key had exactly 
\f4\i one
\f2\i0  owner. Now, for replication factor 
\f3\b N=3
\f2\b0 , every key maps to a 
\f3\b preference list
\f2\b0  of 3 distinct physical nodes (walking clockwise from the key's ring position, same as before, but collecting 3 distinct nodes instead of 1). Whichever node receives the client's request becomes the 
\f3\b coordinator
\f2\b0  for that operation: it fans the request out to all 3 nodes in the preference list (calling itself directly if it happens to be one of them) and waits for enough responses.\

\f3\b Quorum math.
\f2\b0  With N=3, you choose W (write quorum) and R (read quorum) such that 
\f3\b R + W > N
\f2\b0 . We'll use W=2, R=2 (2+2=4 > 3). Why does this work? Any write-set of W nodes and any read-set of R nodes, both drawn from the same N nodes, are guaranteed to overlap in at least 
\f5\fs26 R + W - N
\f2\fs24  nodes \'97 with our numbers, at least 1. That overlap guarantees a read will always see the most recently 
\f4\i acknowledged
\f2\i0  write, as long as fewer than W nodes are simultaneously down.\

\f3\b Versioning (temporary, until Phase 5).
\f2\b0  To know which of several replica responses is "newest," every stored value needs a version marker. Proper Dynamo-style systems use vector clocks (that's Phase 5). For now we'll use a simple 
\f3\b wall-clock timestamp
\f2\b0  attached to every write \'97 this is "last write wins" (LWW). It's a known simplification: if two nodes' clocks are skewed, LWW can pick the wrong winner. Note this limitation now; Phase 5 replaces it with something clock-independent.\

\f3\b Tombstones.
\f2\b0  If 
\f5\fs26 Delete
\f2\fs24  just erased the key from a node's map, a stale replica that missed the delete could "resurrect" the old value during a later read-repair or quorum read. Instead, 
\f5\fs26 Delete
\f2\fs24  writes a special 
\f3\b tombstone
\f2\b0  entry (same timestamp mechanism, marked as deleted) so it participates correctly in LWW comparisons.\

\f3\b Ack vs. applied.
\f2\b0  When a replica receives a write, it should acknowledge success even if it decided (via LWW) to ignore the write because it already had something newer. "Ack" means "I safely and correctly processed your request," not "I changed my data." Conflating these breaks quorum math.\

\f3\b Simplification we're making:
\f2\b0  our implementation will wait for 
\f4\i all
\f2\i0  N replicas to respond (or time out) before checking whether W/R was satisfied, rather than returning as soon as W acks arrive. This is slightly slower than optimal Dynamo behavior but much easier to implement correctly with plain 
\f5\fs26 std::future
\f2\fs24 . Worth knowing as a future optimization.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
