\f3\b\fs24 \cf0 Important nuance about "tolerate up to two node failures without data loss":
\f2\b0  with N=3, W=2, R=2, this is true in terms of 
\f4\i data loss
\f2\i0  \'97 as long as at least 1 of the 3 replicas for a key survives, the data isn't gone. But 
\f4\i availability
\f2\i0  for a specific key is only guaranteed if at most 1 of its 3 replicas is down; if 2 of the 3 nodes holding a particular key die simultaneously, operations on that specific key become temporarily unavailable (correctly refused, not silently wrong) until you have gossip-based failure detection and hinted handoff (later phases) or the nodes come back. Since a 6-node cluster spreads keys across many different 3-node preference lists, killing any 2 of the 6 nodes only affects the subset of keys whose preference list happened to include both \'97 most data stays available. This is worth understanding precisely since it's exactly what your resume bullet is claiming.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
