\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 Phase 3 \'97 Replication and Quorum Consensus\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf0 1. Overall objective of Phase 3\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 In Phase 2, the key-value store had 
\f3\b consistent hashing and routing
\f2\b0 , but each key effectively had one responsible physical node.\
Phase 3 changed the system from a sharded key-value store into a 
\f3\b replicated distributed key-value store
\f2\b0 .\
The main ideas introduced were:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Replication factor:
\f2\b0  
\f5\fs26 N = 3
\f2\fs24 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Write quorum:
\f2\b0  
\f5\fs26 W = 2
\f2\fs24 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Read quorum:
\f2\b0  
\f5\fs26 R = 2
\f2\fs24 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Preference lists
\f2\b0 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Coordinator-based replication
\f2\b0 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Concurrent RPC fan-out using 
\f6\fs26 std::async
\f3\fs24  and 
\f6\fs26 std::future
\f2\b0\fs24 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Timestamp-based versioning
\f2\b0 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Last-Write-Wins (LWW)
\f2\b0 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Tombstones for deletes
\f2\b0 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Internal node-to-node gRPC RPCs
\f2\b0 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Debug RPCs for inspecting internal state
\f2\b0 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Fault-tolerance testing
\f2\b0 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cluster-management Bash scripts
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The fundamental change is that a key is no longer associated with just one node. Its data is replicated across 
\f3\b three distinct physical nodes
\f2\b0 , selected using the consistent-hash ring.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 2. Replication Factor and Preference Lists\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Replication factor N = 3\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Every key now has 
\f3\b three replicas
\f2\b0 .\
The three nodes are determined by the key's position on the consistent-hash ring.\
Instead of:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "Which one node owns this key?"\
the system now asks:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "Which three physical nodes should hold this key?"\
This collection of nodes is called the 
\f3\b preference list
\f2\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Why the key is required\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 The preference list depends on the key because different keys hash to different locations on the ring.\
The algorithm:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Hash the key.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Find its position on the ring.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Start traversing clockwise.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Collect physical nodes encountered.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Skip duplicate physical nodes caused by virtual nodes.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Stop after finding 
\f5\fs26 N = 3
\f2\fs24  distinct physical nodes.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Wrap around to the beginning of the ring if necessary.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 So although the ring contains many 
\f3\b virtual nodes
\f2\b0 , the preference list contains three 
\f3\b distinct physical servers
\f2\b0 .\
The implementation uses a set to ensure that a physical node isn't added twice. It also tracks how much of the ring has been visited so that traversal terminates safely. The final implementation retains the existing 150 virtual nodes per physical node.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f6\b\fs30\fsmilli15210 \cf0 size_t
\f3\fs28  learning\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 During ring traversal, the visited counter is a 
\f5\fs26 size_t
\f2\fs24  because it represents a container size/count and is compared against 
\f5\fs26 ring_.size()
\f2\fs24 , which itself returns 
\f5\fs26 size_t
\f2\fs24 .\
The comparison against 
\f5\fs26 n
\f2\fs24  is explicitly converted to 
\f5\fs26 size_t
\f2\fs24  as well, avoiding signed/unsigned comparison issues.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 3. Quorum Consensus: N = 3, W = 2, R = 2\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is the central distributed-systems concept of Phase 3.\
You have:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 N = 3:
\f2\b0  three replicas\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 W = 2:
\f2\b0  two acknowledgements required for a successful write\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 R = 2:
\f2\b0  two successful responses required for a successful read\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The important relationship is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 R + W > N
\f2\b0 \
Here:\

\f3\b 2 + 2 > 3
\f2\b0 \
Therefore, any successful read quorum and successful write quorum must overlap by at least one replica.\
That overlap is what gives the quorum system its consistency property: a read has a guaranteed chance to see a replica containing the latest acknowledged write.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Why not require all three?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 If all three replicas had to acknowledge every write, losing one node would make the write fail.\
With 
\f5\fs26 W = 2
\f2\fs24 , the system can tolerate 
\f3\b one unavailable replica
\f2\b0  while still accepting writes.\
Similarly, 
\f5\fs26 R = 2
\f2\fs24  means a read can succeed with one replica unavailable.\
But if two of the three replicas are unavailable, neither quorum can be reached.\
This was later explicitly tested.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 4. Coordinator concept\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 A major new idea is the 
\f3\b coordinator
\f2\b0 .\
The coordinator is 
\f3\b not necessarily the node that permanently owns the key
\f2\b0 .\
It is simply the server that receives the client's request.\
For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 Client\
   |\
   v\
Node 5\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 Node 5 becomes the coordinator for that request.\
It then:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Determines the key's preference list.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Sends the operation to the three replicas.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Collects their responses.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Applies quorum rules.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Returns the result to the client.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This means a client can contact essentially any cluster node, and that node can coordinate the operation.\
This builds directly on the Phase 2 routing architecture.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 5. Protobuf/gRPC architecture was extended\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 The original client-facing API remained:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Put
\f2\fs24 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Get
\f2\fs24 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Delete
\f2\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 But Phase 3 added a second category of RPCs: 
\f3\b internal node-to-node replication RPCs
\f2\b0 .\
You added:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 ReplicatePut
\f2\fs24 \
\ls8\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 ReplicateGet
\f2\fs24 \
\ls8\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 ReplicateDelete
\f2\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 These are different from the public client API.\
A coordinator uses these RPCs to communicate with peer replicas.\
For example, conceptually:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 Client\
   |\
   v\
Coordinator\
   |\
   +---- ReplicatePut ---> Replica 1\
   |\
   +---- ReplicatePut ---> Replica 2\
   |\
   +---- local Put ------> Replica 3 (if coordinator is one of them)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 The protobuf messages were also extended to carry timestamps and tombstone information where required. 
\f5\fs26 ReplicateGetResponse
\f2\fs24 , for example, contains the value, timestamp, and tombstone state.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 6. Peer stubs and gRPC channels\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Every server now knows about the other nodes in 
\f5\fs26 cluster.conf
\f2\fs24 .\
When a server starts:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 It adds every configured node to its hash ring.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 For every node other than itself, it creates a gRPC channel.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 It creates a 
\f5\fs26 KeyValueStore::Stub
\f2\fs24  for that peer.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 These stubs are stored in a map indexed by node address.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 A 
\f3\b stub is a local proxy object
\f2\b0  representing the remote service.\
Calling a method on a stub causes gRPC to perform the actual network communication with the corresponding server.\
This gave each server the ability to communicate directly with every other server.\
You also learned an important C++ ownership concept here:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 the stubs are stored as 
\f5\fs26 std::unique_ptr<Stub>
\f2\fs24 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 .get()
\f2\fs24  can obtain a non-owning raw 
\f5\fs26 Stub*
\f2\fs24 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 a 
\f5\fs26 unique_ptr
\f2\fs24  itself cannot simply be copied because it represents unique ownership.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 7. Why the server can handle concurrent requests\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 One important concept you explored during Phase 3 was how a single gRPC server port can handle many clients.\
There is still only one 
\f5\fs26 KVServiceImpl
\f2\fs24  object and one listening server/port, but gRPC can execute RPC handlers concurrently using its internal execution machinery.\
Therefore multiple client requests can enter methods such as 
\f5\fs26 Put
\f2\fs24 , 
\f5\fs26 Get
\f2\fs24 , and 
\f5\fs26 Delete
\f2\fs24  concurrently.\
This explains why your shared objects need synchronization.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Storage engine\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Multiple RPC handlers may simultaneously access:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 StorageEngine\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 so it uses a 
\f5\fs26 shared_mutex
\f2\fs24 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Hash ring\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Multiple requests can simultaneously query the ring, so it also uses a 
\f5\fs26 shared_mutex
\f2\fs24 .\
You therefore learned the distinction between:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Concurrent server method execution
\f2\b0 \
and\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Protected critical sections inside shared data structures.
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 8. Concurrent replication with 
\f6\fs52 std::async
\f3\fs48  and 
\f6\fs52 std::future
\f3\fs48 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 One of the biggest changes in Phase 3 was replacing sequential replica communication with 
\f3\b parallel fan-out
\f2\b0 .\
A na\'efve implementation would do:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 send to replica 1\
wait\
\
send to replica 2\
wait\
\
send to replica 3\
wait\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 This is unnecessarily slow, particularly when one replica is down.\
Instead, the coordinator launches the remote replication calls concurrently.\
Each asynchronous operation produces a:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 future<bool>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 for writes/deletes, or a:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 future<GetReply>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 for reads.\
The futures are stored and later collected.\
The important conceptual distinction you learned is:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 std::async(std::launch::async, ...)
\f2\fs24  starts the work asynchronously.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 std::future
\f2\fs24  represents the eventual result.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 future.get()
\f2\fs24  retrieves the result and waits if necessary.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 So all remote RPCs are started first, and then the coordinator waits for their results.\
The final implementation uses this pattern for 
\f5\fs26 Put
\f2\fs24 , 
\f5\fs26 Get
\f2\fs24 , and 
\f5\fs26 Delete
\f2\fs24 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 9. Lambda captures and the 
\f6\fs52 node
\f3\fs48  issue\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 You spent significant time understanding why the asynchronous lambda captures its data by value.\
The important concept was:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 [node]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 means:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Make a copy of 
\f5\fs26 node
\f2\fs24  inside the lambda.\
Whereas:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 [&node]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 would capture a reference to the loop variable.\
Because the asynchronous task may execute after the loop has advanced, capturing the loop variable by reference can result in observing the wrong iteration's value or, after the loop finishes, a dangling reference.\
You also learned that the explicit 
\f5\fs26 node_copy
\f2\fs24  temporary isn't fundamentally necessary if the lambda simply captures 
\f5\fs26 node
\f2\fs24  by value. The 
\f3\b important property is value capture
\f2\b0 , not the variable's name.\
You also learned 
\f3\b init-capture
\f2\b0 , which lets the lambda create its own variables directly from expressions such as the protobuf request's key and value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 10. 
\f6\fs52 this
\f3\fs48  in lambda capture\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 You also learned why the asynchronous lambdas capture 
\f5\fs26 this
\f2\fs24 .\
The lambda runs inside a member function of 
\f5\fs26 KVServiceImpl
\f2\fs24 , but it needs access to members such as:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 peer_stubs_
\f2\fs24 \
\ls12\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 SendReplicatePut
\f2\fs24 \
\ls12\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 SendReplicateGet
\f2\fs24 \
\ls12\ilvl0
\f5\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 SendReplicateDelete
\f2\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 this
\f2\fs24  is the pointer to the current 
\f5\fs26 KVServiceImpl
\f2\fs24  object.\
Capturing 
\f5\fs26 this
\f2\fs24  therefore allows the asynchronous lambda to access that object's members.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 11. Put coordinator logic\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 A 
\f5\fs26 Put
\f2\fs24  now follows this conceptual process:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Step 1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Generate a timestamp at the coordinator.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Step 2\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Find the three-node preference list.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Step 3\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 For every replica:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If the replica is the coordinator itself, perform the local storage operation.\
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Otherwise, start an asynchronous 
\f5\fs26 ReplicatePut
\f2\fs24  RPC.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Step 4\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Wait for all futures.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Step 5\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Count successful acknowledgements.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Step 6\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Compare the count with 
\f5\fs26 W = 2
\f2\fs24 .\
If at least two replicas acknowledge, the client receives a successful write.\
Otherwise the coordinator returns 
\f5\fs26 UNAVAILABLE
\f2\fs24 .\
The final code follows this fan-out and quorum structure.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 12. Important distinction: ACK vs. value actually changing\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 You learned a subtle but important distributed-systems concept here.\
A replica receiving a replication request does 
\f3\b not
\f2\b0  necessarily mean that its stored value changed.\
Suppose the replica already contains:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 timestamp = 500\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 and receives:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 timestamp = 400\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 The storage engine rejects the stale write.\
But the replica can still acknowledge:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "I successfully received and processed your replication request."\
That is different from:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "My stored value changed."\
This distinction prevents an otherwise healthy replica with newer data from being incorrectly treated as a failed replica and causing quorum calculations to fail.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 13. Internal 
\f6\fs52 ReplicatePut
\f3\fs48 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 The internal replication handler is deliberately 
\f3\b passive
\f2\b0 .\
It does not:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 calculate the preference list,\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 initiate another round of replication,\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 calculate a quorum,\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 become a coordinator.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 It simply:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls15\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 receives the key, value, and timestamp,\
\ls15\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 passes them to its local storage engine,\
\ls15\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 acknowledges that the request was processed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This prevents replication from becoming recursive:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 Coordinator\
 \uc0\u8594  Replica\
   \uc0\u8594  Replica\
     \uc0\u8594  Replica\
       \uc0\u8594  ...\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 The coordinator is responsible for coordination; replicas simply execute the internal request.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 14. Read / Get coordinator logic\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Reads are more complicated than writes because the three replicas may temporarily contain different versions.\
The coordinator:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls16\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 obtains the preference list,\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 reads its local replica if applicable,\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 asynchronously sends 
\f5\fs26 ReplicateGet
\f2\fs24  to remote replicas,\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 gathers all replies,\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 ignores failed RPCs,\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 counts successful responses,\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 selects the response with the 
\f3\b highest timestamp
\f2\b0 ,\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	8	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 checks whether that winning version is a tombstone,\
\ls16\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	9	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 returns the appropriate client response.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The 
\f5\fs26 GetReply
\f2\fs24  structure was introduced to keep together:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls17\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 whether the RPC succeeded,\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 whether the key exists,\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 the value,\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 the timestamp,\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 whether the entry is a tombstone.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This makes it possible to distinguish 
\f3\b network failure
\f2\b0 , 
\f3\b missing key
\f2\b0 , 
\f3\b normal value
\f2\b0 , and 
\f3\b deleted value
\f2\b0 .\
The final implementation selects the highest timestamp among successful responses and only reports the key as found when the winning entry is not a tombstone.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 15. Last-Write-Wins versioning\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Phase 3 introduced 
\f3\b versioning
\f2\b0 .\
Previously, storage essentially contained:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 key \uc0\u8594  value\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 Now it contains conceptually:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 key \uc0\u8594  \{\
    value,\
    timestamp,\
    tombstone status\
\}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 The timestamp is generated using the coordinator's wall-clock time at 
\f3\b microsecond resolution
\f2\b0 .\
The important thing you learned is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Microseconds are a unit of timestamp representation, not a statement that the server can process requests only one microsecond apart.\
Two requests can theoretically receive the same timestamp if they happen within the same clock resolution.\
For Phase 3, this is accepted as a temporary simplification.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 16. Why timestamps are needed\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Imagine:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 Replica A \uc0\u8594  value = "old"\
Replica B \uc0\u8594  value = "new"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 A quorum read could receive both.\
Without version information, the coordinator has no way to determine which value should win.\
With timestamps:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 old \uc0\u8594  timestamp 100\
new \uc0\u8594  timestamp 120\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 the coordinator chooses timestamp 120.\
That is 
\f3\b Last-Write-Wins
\f2\b0 .\
The Phase 3 design explicitly treats wall-clock timestamps as a temporary bridge; the project plan intends to replace this with 
\f3\b vector clocks in Phase 5
\f2\b0 , because physical clocks on distributed machines can drift and are not a perfect ordering mechanism.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 17. Storage Engine versioning\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 The storage engine now stores a 
\f5\fs26 VersionedValue
\f2\fs24  containing:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 value\
\ls18\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 timestamp\
\ls18\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 tombstone flag\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The map remains protected by 
\f5\fs26 shared_mutex
\f2\fs24 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Put behavior\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 A new value replaces the existing value only if its timestamp is 
\f3\b strictly newer
\f2\b0 .\
Therefore an older write cannot overwrite newer data.\
This gives you local LWW enforcement even if messages arrive out of order.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Get behavior\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f5\b0\fs26 \cf0 GetVersioned
\f2\fs24  returns the entire versioned record instead of merely the value.\
This is important because the coordinator needs the timestamp and tombstone state to make the distributed read decision.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 18. Tombstones\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 A normal delete cannot simply erase the key.\
Suppose:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 Node A: old value\
Node B: old value\
Node C: old value\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 Then B and C receive a delete and erase their entries.\
But A is offline.\
Later A comes back.\
If its old value still exists, it could reintroduce that supposedly deleted value into the system.\
This is 
\f3\b delete resurrection
\f2\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 Tombstone solution\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Instead of physically removing the entry, deletion creates a special version:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 value = empty\
timestamp = deletion timestamp\
is_tombstone = true\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 The tombstone therefore participates in the same version ordering as ordinary writes.\
An older write cannot overwrite a newer tombstone.\
A newer write can legitimately overwrite the tombstone.\
The storage-engine tests explicitly check this behavior.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

