\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 You also created 
\f5\fs26 fault_tolerance_test.cpp
\f2\fs24 .\
This test deliberately kills nodes from the preference list and observes the resulting quorum behavior.\
The important cases are:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 All 3 replicas alive\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 A write succeeds normally.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 1 replica dead\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Two replicas remain.\
Since:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 W = 2\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 writes can still succeed.\
Similarly:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 R = 2\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 reads can still succeed.\
This demonstrates the actual purpose of replication and quorum consensus.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf0 2 replicas dead\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Only one replica remains.\
Now:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs26 \cf0 1 < W\
1 < R\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 so neither write nor read quorum can be satisfied.\
The test intentionally expects the read to fail rather than silently returning incorrect data.\
This is an important distributed-systems lesson:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Fault tolerance does not mean "the system always works." It means the system continues working within the failure conditions that its quorum configuration can tolerate, and fails explicitly when those conditions are exceeded.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
